    "https://www.lakeheadu.ca/faculty-and-staff/departments/services/hr/employment-opportunities/employment-opportunities-db/node/351833": {
      "title": "NSERC Tier 2 Canada Research Chair (CRC) Nature Inspired Biomimetics and Engineering",
      "description": "NSERC Tier 2 Canada Research Chair (CRC) Nature Inspired Biomimetics and Engineering | Lakehead University LakeheadU.ca Thunder Bay • Orillia • Barrie User Login Search form Search Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Donate Visit Apply Now Romeo Research Library mySuccess myCourseLink myEmail myPortal Students Faculty & Staff Alumni Departments Expand Academic Administrative Offices & Services Directory Forms Database Faculty Resources Expand Curriculum Navigator Guides Helping Students Information for Chairs & Directors Instructional Development How Can I Help a Student Meeting & Conference Space Plans & Performance Measures Expand Accountability Facts & Figures Plans Student Engagement & Satisfaction Surveys Work Study Program Expand LUWSP-FW LUWSP-SS Human Resources Cohort Study Covid-19 Events News Thrive Workload Manageability Contact Us Employee Experience Employment Opportunities Health & Safety Labour Relations Resources for Managers Resources for Staff Our Trail to Wellness Human Resources Home Faculty & Staff Departments Administrative Offices & Services Human Resources . . . Employment Opportunities Employment Opportunities DB Set Campus Menu Human Resources Cohort Study Covid-19 Events News Thrive Workload Manageability Contact Us Employee Experience Employment Opportunities Health & Safety Labour Relations Resources for Managers Resources for Staff Our Trail to Wellness NSERC Tier 2 Canada Research Chair (CRC) Nature Inspired Biomimetics and Engineering Engineering Thunder Bay Full-time Contract Overview Application Deadline:  December 1, 2026 Posting Date: October 6, 2026 Faculty of Engineering Reference #: ENG-2026-27-08 NSERC Tier 2 Canada Research Chair (CRC) Nature Inspired Biomimetics and Engineering Lakehead University, Thunder Bay Campus, invites applications for an NSERC Tier 2 Canada Research Chair in Nature Inspired Biomimetics and Engineering at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor in the Faculty of Engineering. The academic rank will be commensurate with the qualifications of the successful candidate.  The position will commence within four to six months following the CRC notice of approval.  The CRC position is for five (5) years and is renewable once and subject to approval by the Tri-Agency Institutional Programs (TIPs) Secretariat. The Faculty of Engineering The Faculty of Engineering at Lakehead University provides undergraduate and graduate degrees for six branches of engineering and offers professional engineering education in fields of Civil, Chemical, Electrical, Mechanical, Mechatronics, and Software engineering. With strong ties with local industries, many state-of-the-art research laboratories and MSc and PhD programs, the faculty is an attractive host for graduate studies in nature inspired engineering topics. Lakehead University also has several centralized labs to support research, including the Lakehead University Instrumentation Lab. Lakehead University is also a member of the SHARCNET Computing Consortium. For further information about the Faculty of Engineering, please contact Dr. David Barnett, Interim Dean, Faculty of Engineering.  Detailed information on the Departments in Engineering is available through the Faculty of Engineering website . Qualifications The ideal candidate is one who can contribute to the development of nature inspired engineering and/or systems. Researchers with interests in sustainable engineering processes and systems are particularly encouraged to apply. Tier 2 CRCs are intended for outstanding and innovative emerging researchers whose accomplishments have made a major impact in their fields and are recognized as leaders in their fields. The Chair will increase the research capacity and training opportunities in nature derived processes and systems at Lakehead University. The Chair will also add to the continuing development of research excellence within the Faculty of Engineering within the University. Candidates are also expected to demonstrate a strong record of service to the research community (e.g., service on peer review committees, advisory committees), and a demonstrated record of working with and mentoring students, trainees, and research personnel. The candidates are expected to have outstanding track-record in the field of nature inspired materials and/or systems.  Evidence of excellence in teaching will be assessed through teaching accomplishments, and a teaching dossier that can be submitted as part of the application (with required materials outlined below).  Experience with experiential learning and online or alternative modes of delivery is highly desired. Nominees to the CRC Program will develop and implement a plan for establishing and maintaining a diverse team and an inclusive research and training environment that promotes co-creation with partners from all sectors (academic, public, private, not-for-profit and philanthropic), in order to increase research knowledge mobilization and translation for the benefit of all Canadians. In addition, Lakehead University requires that applicants must have earned a PhD degree in Engineering, or a related area. The position requires eligibility for professional engineering licensure in Ontario (PEO), or an equivalent provincial body in Canada; candidates not currently licensed will be expected to achieve licensure within three years of appointment. We seek candidates whose research and teaching interests complement and enhance our existing research strengths. Compensation The expected annual compensation range for this position is: -  Assistant Professor: $88,094 – $133,223 -  Associate Professor: $141,099 – $191,099 Salary will be determined in accordance with collective agreement and will be commensurate with qualifications and experience.  Tier 2 CRCs at Lakehead will receive an additional $5,000 CRC stipend in their first term. CRC Eligibility Tier 2 CRCs are for exceptional emerging researchers, acknowledged by their peers as having the potential to lead in their field. Candidates for Tier 2 Chair positions must meet all the requirements for Tier 2 CRCs and be eligible for assistant or associate professor positions. Tier 2 CRCs normally are not more than 10 years from commencing their first independent academic research-related appointment at the time of submitting the nomination to the CRC Secretariat.  The CRC Nomination will be submitted by April 1, 2027. However, Lakehead University may nominate a professor or a researcher who is more than 10 years from their first independent academic research-related appointment if the candidate has experienced career interruptions based on the categories outlined here . Lakehead University will seek CRC eligibility approval for shortlisted candidates only. Successful candidates will: *  be excellent emerging world-class researchers who have demonstrated research creativity; *  have demonstrated the potential to achieve international recognition in their fields in the next five to ten years; *  as chairholders, have the potential to attract, develop and retain excellent trainees, students, and future researchers; and *  be proposing an original, innovative research program in molecular imaging that is of the highest quality. For the duration of the CRC, a reduced teaching load will allow the Chair to dedicate the majority of their time to research; they will be provided with a start-up grant, CFI-JELF Infrastructure Grant, an annual research grant, and research space appropriate for the proposed CRC’s program of research. Application Requirements and Deadline Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. The electronic application (in the form of one PDF document) quoting Reference # ENG-2026-27-08 should include: A complete application as one electronic pdf file consists of: 1.         Cover letter (up to 2 pages) and letter of interest with a statement confirming eligibility for a Tier 2 CRC (not more than 10 years from commencing their first independent academic research-related appointment at the time of submitting the nomination to the CRC anticipated to be in March 2027. 2.         Detailed curriculum vitae (any format) with link to an up-to-date ORCID profile; 3.         Significant Contributions and Impact (1 page) with three publications included; 4.         Statement of Research Interests and description of Proposed CRC Research Five-Year Research Program (approximately 5 pages); 5.         Statement of Teaching Philosophy (up to 3 pages); 6.         Statement describing the candidate's contributions, or potential contributions to, equity, diversity, and inclusion (EDI) along with their ability to work with a culturally diverse and international student, staff, and faculty body (up to 1 page); and 7.         Name and contact information of three references, at least one of whom must be at arm’s length. Incomplete applications will not be considered.  Once your application is submitted, you will be asked to complete a mandatory brief self-identification survey that is a CRC requirement. The questions in the survey are voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning and program reporting. Applicants should submit their complete electronic application quoting reference number Reference # ENG-2026-27-08 to: Dr. David Barnett, Interim Dean Faculty of Engineering Lakehead University 955 Oliver Road Thunder Bay, ON P7B 5E1 Email to: admin.engineering@lakeheadu.ca Please note that only those selected for an interview will be notified. Please note that this job posting is for an existing CRC vacancy. Lakehead University does not use artificial intelligence (AI) software to screen, assess, or select candidates for this position. The successful candidate will be expected to work with the Office of Research Services in completing a CRC Tier 2 and CFI nomination package expected to be completed within four months of acceptance of the CRC conditional appointment. If you have questions about the CRC program at Lakehead University, please contact Anne Klymenko, Director, Research Services by email: aklymenk@lakeheadu.ca or by telephone at 807-343-8010 Ext. 8223. World-Class Research, Global Impact For 60 years, students and world-class researchers have chosen Lakehead University to drive transformative discovery, address society’s most complex challenges, and shape what’s next. Consistently celebrated for its extraordinary research intensity, Lakehead is ranked the #2 undergraduate research university in Canada by Research Infosource —holding the top spot nationally for not-for-profit research income and leading the country in cross-sector Artificial Intelligence research collaborations. Internationally recognized as a powerhouse for global impact, Lakehead ranks 90th worldwide in the Times Higher Education (THE) Impact Rankings for advancing the UN Sustainable Development Goals, while standing as the #1 university in Canada and North America with under 10,000 students for global impact. Fully aligned with our Strategic Plan, Research Plan, CRC EDI Action Plan, and Institutional EDI Action Plan, we actively invite outstanding scholars to join our vibrant research ecosystem. Nestled in the diverse and culturally rich communities of Thunder Bay, Orillia, and Barrie, our unique campus environments inspire innovative thinking, dynamic community-partnered innovation, and opportunities to drive meaningful, real-world research impact. Lakehead University and our Community Lakehead is a comprehensive university with a reputation for innovative programs and cutting-edge research. Our campuses are located in Thunder Bay on the traditional lands of the Fort William First Nation, Signatory to the Robinson Superior Treaty of 1850 and in Orillia on the traditional territory of the Anishinaabeg, and Rama First Nation. Lakehead University acknowledges the history that many nations hold in the areas around our campuses and is committed to a relationship with Métis and Inuit and First Nations peoples. Lakehead has approximately 10,000 students and 2,160 faculty and staff. With an emphasis on collaborative learning and independent critical thinking and a multidisciplinary teaching approach, Lakehead offers a variety of degree and diploma programs at the undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral levels through its nine faculties, including Business Administration, Education, Engineering, Health and Behavioural Sciences, Natural Resources Management, Science and Environmental Studies, Social Sciences and Humanities, Graduate Studies, and Faculty of Law. For further information, please visit: www.lakeheadu.ca . Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Lakehead University is committed to creating a diverse and inclusive environment and welcomes applications from all qualified individuals including women, racialized persons, Indigenous people, persons with disabilities and other equity-seeking groups. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply. Please note that the CRC Program imposes no restrictions on nominees concerning nationality or country of residence. Lakehead University has an Equity, Diversity and Inclusion Action Plan 2019-2024 with a goal to recruit and retain a diverse workforce as measured by increasing representation of under-represented groups among applicants, candidates and hires. We encourage candidates to self-identify, if you are from an under-represented group, and prefer candidates with the knowledge, competencies and relationships derived from lived experience. Experience working with Indigenous or racialized communities, and/or members of other equity-deserving groups is a strong asset. A lived experience or worked experience of any of these issues is preferred. Lakehead University recognizes that alternative career paths and/or career interruptions (e.g., maternity leave, parental leave, leave due to illness, leaves due to caring for family members, or slowdowns due to chronic illness or disability) can impact research achievements and commits to ensuring that leaves are taken into careful consideration in the assessment of the candidate. Candidates are encouraged to highlight how alternative paths and/or interruptions have impacted their research in their application. Lakehead also recognizes the value of mentoring and research training, outreach, professional service, community-based research, other forms of scholarly expression, and/or research outputs; demonstrated experience in increasing diversity in the previous institutional environment, and in curriculum, is also an asset. Lakehead University recognizes the legitimate impact that leaves (e.g., parental leaves, slowdowns due to illness or disability, etc.) can have on a nominee’s record of research achievement. These leaves will be taken into careful consideration during the review and assessment of application materials. Please visit our Office of Human Rights and Equity website and our Human Resources website with regards to accommodations and to explore all of the supports our organization provides to enable a strong and connected employment experience. Lakehead University is committed to supporting an accessible environment. Applicants requiring accommodation during the interview process should contact Katherine Mends at the Office of Human Resources at (807) 343.8010 ext. 8334 or human.resources@lakeheadu.ca to make appropriate arrangements. Additional Information The Canada Research Chairs Program supports outstanding researchers in areas that will further Lakehead's Strategic Research Plan. All Chair nominations are subject to review and final approval by the CRC Secretariat. Please consult the Canada Research Chairs website f or full program information and further details on eligibility criteria. The Lakehead University CRC EDI Action Plan is available here and information regarding our EDI policies can be found here . AI may or may not be used for the screening purposes of this role. Lakehead University is committed to creating a diverse and inclusive environment and welcomes applications from all qualified individuals including women, racialized persons, Indigenous people, persons with disabilities, and other equity-seeking groups. All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadian citizens and permanent residents will be given priority. This is in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements. Lakehead University has a goal to recruit and retain a diverse workforce as measured by increasing representation of underrepresented groups among applicants, candidates, and hires. Experience working with Indigenous or racialized communities, and/or members of other equity-deserving groups, is a strong asset. A lived experience or worked experience of any of these issues is preferred. We appreciate your interest; Lakehead University is committed to supporting an accessible environment. Applicants requiring accommodation during the interview process should contact the Office of Human Resources at (807) 343-8010 ext. 8334 or human.resources@lakeheadu.ca to make appropriate arrangements. Yes, eligible for benefits Contact Information: humanres@lakeheadu.ca Academic Calendar myCourselink Library myEmail myPortal Directory A-Z Index Contact Us Careers Accessibility Privacy Lakehead Thunder Bay Campus Status: Open 955 Oliver Rd Thunder Bay, ON, P7B 5E1 (807) 343.8110 tbay@lakeheadu.ca Lakehead Orillia Campus Status: Open 500 University Avenue Orillia, ON, L3V 0B9 (705) 330.4008 orillia@lakeheadu.ca Barrie STEM Hub Campus Status: Open 5 Ross St. Barrie, ON L4M 3A5 Land Acknowledgment Lakehead University respectfully acknowledges its campuses are located on the traditional lands of Indigenous Peoples. Lakehead Thunder Bay is located on the traditional lands of the Fort William First Nation, Signatory to the Robinson Superior Treaty of 1850 . Lakehead Orillia is located on the traditional territory of the Anishinaabeg. The Anishinaabeg include the Ojibwe, Odawa, and Pottawatomi nations, collectively known as the Three Fires Confederacy. Lakehead University acknowledges the history that many nations hold in the areas around our campuses, and is committed to a relationship with First Nations, Métis, and Inuit Peoples based on the principles of mutual trust, respect, reciprocity, and collaboration in the spirit of reconciliation. © 2026 Lakehead University. All Rights Reserved. Instagram LinkedIn Twitter YouTube Facebook Back to Top"
    }
  },
